{%- import "macros.njk" as m -%}

<main class="page">

  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Pollen and air quality, today</h1>
        <p>Respira shows the pollen levels and the air quality index for where you are, with a multi-day forecast and a seasonal calendar by species.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          {{ m.storeBadge(i18nWeb.en) }}
          <span class="hero-cta-note">{{ i18nWeb.en.badge.lead }}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-shot">
        <img src="/assets/img/app-en-01.webp" width="600" height="1298"
             alt="Respira's home screen showing the overall pollen level, today's allergens and air quality for Minneapolis."
             fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="countries">
    <h2 id="countries">Eight countries, two data sources</h2>
    <p>In the <strong>United States</strong>, Respira uses the Google Pollen API: eleven species, updated daily, for wherever you are. In <strong>Spain, Portugal, France, Italy, Germany, Ireland and the United Kingdom</strong>, it uses the Copernicus CAMS model, enriched with the national monitoring networks of Spain, Italy and Germany where they publish measured readings.</p>
    <p>Which species you are offered depends on where you are, because the honest answer differs: ragweed, oak and mountain cedar matter in the United States and olive and pellitory do not. Respira never shows a level for a species its source does not report there.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cities">
    <h2 id="cities">Pollen by city</h2>
    <p>Every US metro has a different pollen year: ragweed defines late summer in Minneapolis, mountain cedar defines December in Austin, and alder starts in February in Seattle before anywhere else has begun.</p>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {%- for c in usCities | slice(1) | first %}{% if loop.index <= 12 %}
      <li><a href="/en/pollen-{{ c.slug }}">{{ c.name }}, {{ c.abbr }}</a></li>
      {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/en/pollen-map-usa">All 50 cities</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="allergens">
    <h2 id="allergens">Allergens in the United States</h2>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {%- for info in usPollenInfoList %}
      <li><a href="/en/allergy-{{ info.slug }}">{{ info.h1 | replace(" allergy", "") | replace(" pollen", "") }}</a></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What the app does</h2>
    <p><strong>Today's levels, by species.</strong> Only the allergens you have selected, only where they are actually reported.</p>
    <p><strong>Air quality on the right scale.</strong> The EPA index in the United States, the European index elsewhere &mdash; 49 is "Moderate" on one and "Good" on the other, so showing the wrong one would be worse than showing none.</p>
    <p><strong>A symptom diary.</strong> Record how you felt; the app pairs it with the pollen that day. Export a report for your allergist as a PDF.</p>
    <p><strong>Smart notifications and a trip planner</strong> are part of Respira Pro. Set a threshold per allergen and get told the morning it is crossed, in any of your saved places.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="honest">
    <h2 id="honest">When we have no data, we say so</h2>
    <p>Pollen models have gaps &mdash; a species with no coverage, a source that did not answer, a cell outside the model's domain. Respira shows "no reading" for those rather than a zero. "No pollen" is a measurement; "we have no data" is not, and the two should never look the same on a screen you are using to decide whether to go outside.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    {{ m.ctaApp("Download Respira free", "Pollen levels for where you are, a multi-day forecast, air quality and a symptom diary. Free, for iPhone and iPad.", i18nWeb.en) }}
  </section>

  <p class="fine-print">Respira is an informational tool. It does not replace diagnosis or advice from a medical professional.</p>
</main>

